# news-radar keyword groups.
#
# A blank line separates one group from the next. Each group is matched,
# capped and displayed on its own.
#
#   word        match if the title contains this term
#   +word       required - the title must contain this too
#   !word       excluded - a title containing this never matches this group
#   @n          keep at most n items in this group, after ranking
#   /pattern/   match by regular expression, against the ORIGINAL title
#   => Label    display name for the group
#   # ...       comment
#
# The FIRST plain term of a group is its primary term: that is the one
# substituted into the search templates in config.yaml. Later plain terms
# widen local matching but cost no extra request.
#
# A group may have NO plain term, if it has at least one /regex/ and a
# `=> Label`. The label is then what the search templates query - the only
# way to hunt for a term without also matching it loosely. The GitHub Trending
# group at the end of this file is the worked example.
#
# Matching folds case and Vietnamese diacritics, so "dien tu" matches
# "Dien tu" and "Điện tử". A /regex/ is applied to the original title.

ESP32
ESP-IDF
ESP32-S3
ESP32-C6
@12
=> ESP32

# The other half of "firmware for ESP and STM". A group of its own rather than
# four more terms in the ESP32 group, because the FIRST plain term is what the
# search templates query and a second family in the same group would never be
# asked for. Measured 2026-09-12: with no query of its own, STM32 appeared in
# 3 of 1269 stored items - the same shape as DeepSeek before it was given a
# group (7 mentions without a query, 67 with one).
#
# `ST` alone is not here and must not be: matching is substring, so it would
# hit almost every title in the file.
STM32
STM32Cube
STM32CubeIDE
STM32H7
STM32F4
STMicroelectronics
!tuyen dung
!hiring
@10
=> STM32

# `firmware` is the widest word in this file and it does not belong to
# embedded: measured 2026-09-12 across 1269 stored items, the group matched 94
# and **36 of them were consumer-device firmware** - Nikon ZR 2.00 in eight
# different spellings, Canon EOS R5, a Sony car stereo, PlayStation 5 14.0,
# Beats 360, an Apple 140 W power adapter.
#
# The exclusions below are that measured list and nothing speculative. They are
# on the group rather than in [GLOBAL_FILTER] on purpose: an ESP32-CAM project
# is a real story, and it still reaches the reader under the ESP32 group, which
# sits ahead of this one.
#
# What this deliberately costs: a headline like `Building Firmware with CMake`
# survives, but `Nikon ZR firmware brings open gate` does not - and neither
# does an ESP32 camera build, which loses its place here and keeps the one it
# already had above.
firmware
bootloader
OTA update
!nikon
!canon
!sony
!fujifilm
!panasonic
!gopro
!dji
!playstation
!ps5
!xbox
!airpods
!beats
!soundbar
!camera
!lens
!car stereo
!power adapter
!smartwatch
!tuyen dung
!hiring
@10
=> Firmware

RISC-V
@8
=> RISC-V

# New models, which is a different question from news about AI. It cannot be
# written as plain terms: `release` matches every ESP-IDF tag and every camera
# firmware note, and `model` matches half the industry. Two regexes instead,
# both run against the ORIGINAL title so the capitals and the version numbers
# do the work:
#
#   1. a versioned model name - the way a release is actually announced.
#   2. a release verb sitting next to a model word, for the launches whose
#      headline names no version.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12 over 1269 stored items: the first matched 80 titles, the
# second 31, and `ESP-IDF Release v5.2.8` and `Canon Announces Firmware
# Updates` matched neither.
#
# `!leak` and `!rumor` are here because the second regex is broad enough to
# pull in the speculation channels that repackage every launch. `!firmware` is
# there for a sharper reason, found in the same measurement: `Amazfit releases
# major firmware update for three T-Rex models` satisfies regex 2 exactly -
# a release verb, then the word `models`, meaning watch models. A model release
# is never a firmware story, and the Firmware group above is where that one
# belongs.
#
# It sits ahead of every vendor group and of AI: a story about Qwen3.8 Max is
# in all three, and `notify.pick()` sends it under the first, where the heading
# says what actually happened.
/(?i)\b(GPT-\d|Claude \d|Gemini \d|Llama \d|Qwen ?[\d.]+|DeepSeek[- ][VR]\d|Grok \d|Gemma \d|Phi-\d|Mistral \w+|Kimi [A-Z]?\d|Ling[- ]\d)/
/(?i)\b(releases?|releasing|launch(?:es|ed|ing)?|unveil(?:s|ed|ing)?|introduc(?:es|ed|ing)|announc(?:es|ed|ing)|debuts?|open[- ]sources?|open[- ]weights?)\b[^\n]{0,60}\b(model|models|LLM|LLMs|checkpoint|weights|parameters)\b/
!leak
!rumor
!firmware
!tuyen dung
!hiring
@10
=> AI Model Release

# One group per model vendor, and each one earns its place by being its own
# search query: a group's first plain term is what the templates ask for, so
# five names in one group would have queried only the first. Measured
# 2026-09-07 on the fixed feeds alone, without a query of their own, GLM
# appeared once, Qwen twice and DeepSeek seven times in 2,239 items - with a
# query each they return 8, 20 and 67. A broad query instead of five narrow
# ones was tried and rejected: "chatbot" returned 36 vendor mentions but only
# ChatGPT and Claude, "LLM" returned 6.
#
# Plain terms, no regexes, because the corpus decided it. `Claude` was the
# worry - Monet, Shannon, Jean-Claude - and `GLM` the other one, being also the
# OpenGL maths library and the generalized linear model. Measured across 2,655
# live items: 135 hits for Claude and 11 for GLM, and every single one was
# about the model. A word-boundary regex found exactly the same set, so it
# would have bought nothing; `!` exclusions can be added the day that changes.
#
# These sit ahead of the AI group deliberately. A story matches both, the page
# shows it in both, but a message sends it once under the first group in this
# file - and "Claude" says more than "AI".
Claude
@6
=> Claude

ChatGPT
@6
=> ChatGPT

GLM
@6
=> GLM

Qwen
@6
=> Qwen

DeepSeek
@6
=> DeepSeek

# "AI" as a plain term would be a disaster: matching is substring, not
# word-boundary, so it would hit said, maintain, chain, fail, email, training
# and Ukraine. Hence the long phrases below plus one regex - a /regex/ is run
# against the ORIGINAL title, so a word boundary and the capitals do the work
# no plain term can.
#
# The first plain term is also the search query. Quoted as a phrase,
# "artificial intelligence" returned 6 relevant hits out of 6 on HN Algolia,
# where a bare LLM returned 3 of 6.
artificial intelligence
tri tue nhan tao
machine learning
deep learning
neural network
large language model
generative AI
OpenAI
Anthropic
DeepSeek
Hugging Face
chatbot
/(\bAI\b|\bA\.I\.|\bLLMs?\b|\bGPT\b|\bAGI\b|\bRAG\b)/
!tuyen dung
!hiring
@12
=> AI

# Projects, not commentary. "open source AI" as a phrase query returned 6 of 6
# actual open-source AI projects on HN Algolia, several of them Show HN posts -
# which the regex catches directly, since Show HN is how a repo announces
# itself. Hyphenated and unhyphenated are two different substrings after
# folding, so both are listed.
open source AI
open-source AI
open source LLM
open-source LLM
self-hosted LLM
awesome-llm
/^Show HN:.*(\bAI\b|\bLLMs?\b|\bGPT\b|\bRAG\b|\bagents?\b)/
!tuyen dung
!hiring
@10
=> AI Repos

# GitHub trending repositories, from the gh_trending feed, which titles every
# entry `owner/repo` and is the only feed whose match_excerpt is on. This group
# is regex-only on purpose: the repo NAME is all the title holds, and the
# description that the excerpt carries is matched by the AI group anyway - 9 of
# 10 trending repos landed there and were then cut, because a feed with no
# dates scores 0.50 at best and the AI group cuts at 0.55. Given its own group
# they compete only with each other.
#
# The regex matches a bare `owner/repo` and nothing else: measured
# 2026-09-07 it caught 18 of 18 trending entries and 0 of 2,300 items across
# the other eleven sources. It ends in a lookahead rather than a plain `$`
# because this feed sets match_excerpt, so the text a regex is shown is the
# title, a newline, then the description - a `$` anchor matched the title on
# its own and nothing at all once the description was appended, which cost
# this group every one of its entries the first time it ran.
#
# The label is the search query, so the templates ask for "GitHub Trending" -
# three extra requests a cycle. Their answers never reach *this* group, because
# an article about trending repos is not titled `owner/repo`, but they are not
# wasted either: measured 2026-09-07, the two Google News queries returned 3
# items and 0 matches, while HN Algolia returned 20 of which 4 matched - all of
# them into AI and AI Repos, `Show HN: Understand GitHub Trending with AI` among
# them. Budget the three requests, do not expect them back as trending repos.
/^[\w.-]+\/[\w.-]+(?=\n|$)/
@8
=> GitHub Trending

# Applied to every item from every source, before any group is considered.
#
# The second block is measured, not guessed: on 2026-09-12 the model-vendor
# groups were carrying Zcash whale trades, SK Hynix share moves and DeepSeek
# IPO filings - a vendor name in a market story is still a market story.
#
# Two words that look like they belong here and must never be added, because
# matching is substring and not word-boundary:
#   !crypto  would also block cryptography, cryptographic, crypto-chip.
#   !ipo     would also block LiPo, which is every battery story in the file.
[GLOBAL_FILTER]
!giveaway
!coupon
!khuyen mai
!sponsored
!zcash
!bitcoin
!ethereum
!memecoin
!altcoin
!cryptocurrency
!share price
!stock market
!whale short
!millionaire
